---
import { type CollectionEntry, getCollection } from "astro:content";

import LessonReferencePage from "@/layouts/LessonReferencePage.astro";

type Course = CollectionEntry<"courses">;

type Props = {
  course: Course;
};

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const courses = await getCollection("courses");

  return courses
    .filter((course) => course.data.slug === "dependency-injection")
    .map((course) => ({
      params: { course: course.data.slug },
      props: { course },
    }));
}

const { course } = Astro.props;
---

<LessonReferencePage
  course={course}
  description="A short, curated reading list for dependency injection, inversion of control, dependency inversion, TypeScript, frontend architecture, and test doubles."
  eyebrow="Reference · further reading"
  title="Curated Dependency Injection resources"
>
  <p>
    Start with the theory sources, then choose the TypeScript, frontend, or
    testing material that matches your question. The list is intentionally short
    and focused on the framework-free frontend scope of this course.
  </p>

  <p class="reference-note">
    The first lesson's recommended path is the
    <a
      href="https://medium.com/ssense-tech/dependency-injection-vs-dependency-inversion-vs-inversion-of-control-lets-set-the-record-straight-5dc818dc32d1"
    >
      SSENSE Tech TypeScript refactor
    </a>
    followed by Fowler's explanations of DI and IoC. Return to the
    <a href={`/courses/${course.data.slug}/glossary/`}>course glossary</a>
    when the terms begin to overlap.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="theory-title">
    <h2 id="theory-title">Theory</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/injection.html"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Inversion of Control Containers and the Dependency Injection pattern,
          Martin Fowler
        </a>. This article named DI. It covers constructor and setter injection,
        DI versus Service Locator, and separating configuration from use.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/InversionOfControl.html"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          InversionOfControl, Martin Fowler
        </a>. This short article explains the "Hollywood Principle" and IoC.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/dipInTheWild.html"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          DIP in the Wild, Brett L. Schuchert
        </a>. It separates DI, IoC, and DIP as wiring, direction, and shape.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://butunclebob.com/ArticleS.UncleBob.PrinciplesOfOod"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          The Principles of OOD, Robert C. Martin
        </a>. This is the source of the "depend on abstractions, not
        concretions" wording.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://livebook.manning.com/book/dependency-injection-principles-practices-patterns/chapter-1/"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Dependency Injection Principles, Practices, and Patterns, chapter 1,
          Seemann and van Deursen
        </a>. It covers composition-root practice and Pure DI in a free sample.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="typescript-title">
    <h2 id="typescript-title">TypeScript</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/arista/typescriptDependencyInjection/blob/master/ManualDependencyInjection.md"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          TypeScript Patterns for Manual Dependency Injection, Arista
        </a>. It covers framework-free constructor injection, contracts,
        hand-written wiring, and test substitution.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://medium.com/ssense-tech/dependency-injection-vs-dependency-inversion-vs-inversion-of-control-lets-set-the-record-straight-5dc818dc32d1"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Dependency Injection vs Dependency Inversion vs Inversion of Control,
          SSENSE Tech
        </a>. It walks through a TypeScript refactor from coupling to injection
        and DIP.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="frontend-title">
    <h2 id="frontend-title">Frontend frameworks</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://angular.dev/guide/di"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Dependency injection in Angular, Angular documentation
        </a>. This shows services, injection contexts, and test doubles in a
        framework.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://angular.dev/guide/di/defining-dependency-providers"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Defining dependency providers, Angular documentation
        </a>. It lists provider levels and <code>useClass</code>,
        <code>useValue</code>, and <code>useFactory</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://angular.dev/guide/di/hierarchical-dependency-injection"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Hierarchical injectors, Angular documentation
        </a>. It explains scoped provider resolution in Angular.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://react.dev/learn/passing-data-deeply-with-context"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Passing data deeply with Context, React documentation
        </a>. It describes Context as a delivery channel and when simpler props
        are enough.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="testing-title">
    <h2 id="testing-title">Testing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://vitest.dev/guide/mocking"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Mocking, Vitest documentation
        </a>. It covers spies, fakes, and mocks when a hand-written fake is not
        enough.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a
          href="https://kentcdodds.com/blog/stop-mocking-fetch"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          target="_blank"
        >
          Stop mocking fetch, Kent C. Dodds
        </a>. It explains why a small fake can keep a browser test focused on
        application logic.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="resources-scope-title" class="source-section">
    <h2 id="resources-scope-title">How to use this list</h2>
    <p>
      Read the lesson first, then use the glossary to check vocabulary. The
      resources are further study, not prerequisites for opening the next
      lesson. This public page contains curated learner-facing links only; it
      does not publish private authoring notes or learning records.
    </p>
  </section>
</LessonReferencePage>
